## Why This Is Asked

This tests ability to design a policy-driven routing component at the edge of LLM ops, focusing on simple data modeling, privacy-conscious preprocessing, and a lightweight test strategy appropriate for beginners.

## Key Concepts

- Policy schema and routing logic
- Per-tenant redaction rules for privacy
- Lightweight test plan (unit + integration)
- Data models and minimal API surface

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight routing sketch
function route(req, policy){
  const variant = policy.allowedVariants.find(v => estimateLatency(v) <= policy.latencyBudgetMs) || 'balanced';
  const redacted = redact(req.prompt, policy.redactFields);
  return { variant, prompt: redacted };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would policy updates propagate without downtime?
- How would you simulate latency and privacy tests in CI?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to architect a real-time, multi-tenant streaming gateway with strict delivery guarantees, policy enforcement, and resilience under failure scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time streaming and exactly-once processing
- Per-tenant policy evaluation and data locality
- Backpressure, circuit breakers, and fault isolation
- Multi-variant routing (base, tuned, safety-guarded)
- Chaos engineering and testability

## Code Example

```javascript
// processPrompt(tenantId, prompt) -> Promise<Response>
async function processPrompt(tenantId, prompt) {
  const policy = await policyService.get(tenantId);
  if (!policy.allow) throw new Error('Policy denied');
  const variant = selectVariant(tenantId, prompt, policy);
  return router.streamPrompt(tenantId, prompt, variant);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you refresh per-tenant policies without restart?
- How would you simulate multi-region chaos in CI/CD runs?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes cost-aware decision making in a multi-tenant LLM gateway, requiring concrete budgeting, latency-based routing, and a test plan. It matches beginner levels but with real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant daily token budget
- Latency thresholds for model selection
- Simple routing policy and observability
- Minimal test plan

## Code Example
```javascript
function routeToModel(tenant, latencyMs, remainingTokens){
  const budgetFrac = remainingTokens / tenant.dailyBudget;
  if (budgetFrac > 0.5 && latencyMs < 80) return 'fast';
  if (latencyMs < 120) return 'balanced';
  return 'accurate';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle budget bursts or resets?
- How would you test correctness under concurrency?
